# THE LAYERS WHOSE WINDOW PERMITS DERIVING A TIME SLACK FROM A CLEARANCE. One row per
# nameplate whose duty cycle leaves the spare spread evenly across the denominator.
#
# ⭐⭐ THIS IS THE POSITIVE FORM OF A RULE USUALLY WRITTEN AS A NEGATION, and the positive
#    form is the one that reads. `timeSlack = q / clearance` assumes the spare is spread
#    evenly across the denominator. A window that is a PROPER PART of the period says it is
#    not -- the machine runs 02:00 to 05:00 -- so the division is over a base that does not
#    exist. `unmeasured` says nobody knows whether it is, which is an assumption wearing a
#    computation's clothes.
#    ⛔ So exactly two REASONS license the derivation -- "it runs the whole period" and "the
#      question has no denominator" -- across however many layers file one of them. Two
#      answers, not two documents.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND THE FIRST OF THE TWO MUST NOT BE READ OUT OF AN ABSENCE, WHICH IS WHY THIS FILE
#    EXISTS. `window_absent IN ('none', 'notApplicable')` reads `none` as
#    "the duty fraction is one" -- a number, filed as though it were a nothing. The absence
#    arm carries no size and no origin, so a line that cannot be stopped and a line somebody
#    staffed round the clock licensed this derivation identically and neither said so. Now
#    the licence is read off a FILED window, and the two spellings collapse into one.
#
# ⭐ THE PERIOD ARRIVES BY A SEMI-JOIN, WHICH IS NOW A CHOICE RATHER THAN A GUARD. This asks
#    "is there a period equal to this window's unit" and uses no value from the answer, so
#    `EXISTS` states the question it means. ⚠️ It started as a guard: `units/with_a_period.sqlc`
#    read `pm.claim.owns`, which carried an element NAME and not a PATH, so `Demand/amount` and
#    `Nameplate/amount` were one `pm:amount` to it and it returned two rows per layer. A plain
#    join fanned out; a semi-join hid it, which is how the fault survived in its only other
#    consumer. `owns` is a path now and the relation is one row per layer.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ A WHOLE PERIOD IS QUOTED AS `1` IN THE PERIOD'S OWN UNIT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
#    TRICK. `1 week` against a period of `week` is a duty fraction of one you can read
#    without dividing anything; `5 days` against the same period is a proper part. No unit
#    conversion happens here on purpose -- `window_not_applicable_on_a_rate` records what
#    happened the last time this directory guessed at units in SQL, and a licence decided by
#    a `LIKE` is a reader's judgement wearing a query's clothes.
-- pm:Divisibility/window: absent notApplicable, or filed as one whole period.
SELECT w.filing, w.layer,
       CASE WHEN w.window_absent IS NOT NULL THEN 'the question has no denominator'
            ELSE 'it runs the whole period' END AS licensed_because
FROM (
    :compose(layers/windows.sqlc)
) w
WHERE w.window_absent = 'notApplicable'
   OR (w.window_low = 1 AND w.window_low = w.window_high
       AND EXISTS (
           SELECT 1
           FROM (
               :compose(units/with_a_period.sqlc)
           ) p
           WHERE p.filing = w.filing AND p.layer = w.layer
             AND p.period = w.window_unit
       ))
